  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "One stroke on top of another; compare 下 (below)"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Originally a small line or dot above a longer line, indicating \"up\". The vertical line was added to distinguish the character from 二 (two)."
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Ideogram (指事): a line above another (contrast 下 (xià)) — up. Originally written as 𠄞, but changed to this form to prevent confusion with 二 (èr).\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *tVŋ (“top”). Cognate with Tibetan སྟེང (steng), Tibetan ཡང་རྩེ (yang rtse, “summit; pinnacle”) (Bodman, 1980), Mizo zo (“to be high”), zah (“to respect; to revere”) (STEDT).\nShijing rimes indicate occasional level tone for pronunciation 2 (Schuessler, 2007).\nPronunciation 1 is the exopassive of pronunciation 2 with suffix *-s, literally meaning \"what has been raised\" (Schuessler, 2007). The rising tone in Middle Chinese words with voiced stop initials regularly evolves into departing tone in Mandarin. Pronunciation 3 results from 叶韻 (xiéyùn), i.e. its pronunciation has been artificially changed to preserve rhymes in classical poetry."
    }
  ],
